{"version":1,"slug":"seasons","topic":"Why do we have seasons?","level":"beginner","title":"Why Do We Have Seasons?","summary":"You will understand how Earth's tilt, not its distance from the sun, creates summer and winter.","scopeNote":null,"scenes":[{"id":"intro","kind":"intro","title":"Why do we have seasons?","segments":[{"id":"intro-0","narration":"Here is a weird puzzle: Earth is actually closest to the sun in January, right in the middle of winter. So why is it freezing?","sig":"O_BM-X0VFrr0KYKRPsmv1HKclVBzJYEs","actions":[{"id":"hero","kind":"icon","cue":"Here is a","text":"Our Tilted Earth","items":null,"icon":"earth","at":{"col":0,"row":1,"w":4,"h":4},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null}]},{"id":"intro-1","narration":"Here's our plan. First, The Distance Myth. Then, Tilt Changes Sunlight. And finally, Opposite Hemispheres.","sig":"R7ugA4EMbWb7FoRHGT2XCg6MuOAGf1f1","actions":[{"id":"agenda","kind":"list","cue":"Here's our plan","text":null,"items":["The Distance Myth","Tilt Changes Sunlight","Opposite Hemispheres"],"icon":null,"at":{"col":5,"row":1,"w":7,"h":4},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null}]}]},{"id":"c1","kind":"chapter","title":"The Distance Myth","segments":[{"id":"c1-0","narration":"Many people think summer is warmer because Earth is closer to the sun, and winter is colder when we are farther away.","sig":"iWvpg5ipsM7IHKfOihoLTz1-9OrfaCx6","actions":[{"id":"myth","kind":"box","cue":"summer is warmer","text":"Closer = Summer","items":null,"icon":null,"at":{"col":7,"row":1,"w":4,"h":1},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"red","expr":null},{"id":"earth","kind":"icon","cue":"because Earth is","text":"Earth","items":null,"icon":"earth","at":{"col":4,"row":1,"w":2,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null},{"id":"sun","kind":"icon","cue":"closer to the sun","text":"Sun","items":null,"icon":"sun","at":{"col":1,"row":1,"w":2,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"orange","expr":null}]},{"id":"c1-1","narration":"It sounds like a great guess, just like standing near a campfire, but it is actually a myth.","sig":"Ceuf6LQjfNTP7T406-zXqjl7xjqxG2Dp","actions":[{"id":"x1","kind":"cross_out","cue":"actually a myth","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"myth","color":"red","expr":null}]},{"id":"c1-2","narration":"In fact, Earth is slightly closer to the sun in January, right in the middle of northern winter.","sig":"qYkPIm6lRK_F4meqFpK6_YvhiGPvW3q5","actions":[{"id":"a1","kind":"arrow","cue":"closer to the sun","text":"January","items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":"sun","to":"earth","target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null},{"id":"p1","kind":"pulse","cue":"northern winter","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"earth","color":"blue","expr":null}]},{"id":"c1-3","narration":"The real reason for seasons is that Earth is tilted by twenty three point five degrees.","sig":"hYXHqIKObrcimLVbEMzEV1tutuJUdSfF","actions":[{"id":"tilt","kind":"number","cue":"Earth is tilted","text":"23.5°","items":["Earth's tilt"],"icon":null,"at":{"col":7,"row":3,"w":4,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"green","expr":null},{"id":"c1","kind":"check","cue":"twenty three point five","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"tilt","color":"green","expr":null}]}]},{"id":"c2","kind":"chapter","title":"Tilt Changes Sunlight","segments":[{"id":"c2-0","narration":"So if distance doesn't cause seasons, what does Earth's tilt actually do? Picture the sun shining on two parts of Earth.","sig":"-qGWYGAOoeADA3tloCwtnzzBdOg1Cst4","actions":[{"id":"sun","kind":"icon","cue":"sun shining","text":"Sun","items":null,"icon":"sun","at":{"col":0,"row":2,"w":2,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"orange","expr":null},{"id":"summer_earth","kind":"icon","cue":"two parts of Earth","text":"Summer","items":null,"icon":"earth","at":{"col":4,"row":0,"w":2,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"green","expr":null},{"id":"winter_earth","kind":"icon","cue":"two parts of Earth","text":"Winter","items":null,"icon":"earth","at":{"col":4,"row":4,"w":2,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null}]},{"id":"c2-1","narration":"When your part tilts toward the sun, sunlight hits straight on, packing lots of energy into a small spot.","sig":"MKwKT3YaiLlCJWJBwnQMNjdcR1BXcdgD","actions":[{"id":"a_sum","kind":"arrow","cue":"hits straight on","text":"Direct","items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":"sun","to":"summer_earth","target":null,"color":"orange","expr":null},{"id":"t_sum","kind":"trace","cue":"packing lots of energy","text":null,"items":null,"icon":"zap","at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"a_sum","color":"orange","expr":null},{"id":"p_sum","kind":"pulse","cue":"a small spot","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"summer_earth","color":"green","expr":null}]},{"id":"c2-2","narration":"When it tilts away, the same sunlight hits at a slant, spreading out over a wider area so it feels much cooler.","sig":"turcITc6vw2DIBDW0jE6bH4zjI22_RxX","actions":[{"id":"a_win","kind":"arrow","cue":"hits at a slant","text":"Slanted","items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":"sun","to":"winter_earth","target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null},{"id":"t_win","kind":"trace","cue":"spreading out","text":null,"items":null,"icon":"zap","at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"a_win","color":"blue","expr":null},{"id":"c_win","kind":"callout","cue":"a wider area","text":"Spreads out","items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"winter_earth","color":"blue","expr":null}]},{"id":"c2-3","narration":"Plus, that tilt gives you longer days in summer and shorter days in winter, which adds even more warmth.","sig":"HfQG4uCVEVGGjX-zoEDFz8WXnxvY36Uv","actions":[{"id":"daylight","kind":"bars","cue":"longer days in summer","text":"Daylight Hours","items":["Summer: 15 hrs","Winter: 9 hrs"],"icon":null,"at":{"col":7,"row":1,"w":5,"h":4},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"orange","expr":null},{"id":"hl_sum","kind":"highlight","cue":"even more warmth","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"summer_earth","color":"green","expr":null}]}]},{"id":"c3","kind":"chapter","title":"Opposite Hemispheres","segments":[{"id":"c3-0","narration":"Now that we know tilt controls sunlight, let us see what happens as Earth orbits the sun. Earth's tilt stays pointed the same way all year long.","sig":"jJWeXDKnoCynHcWmpv2Ah0l3-Cn_53xT","actions":[{"id":"orbit","kind":"cycle","cue":"Earth orbits the sun","text":"Earth's Orbit","items":["June","September","December","March"],"icon":null,"at":{"col":3,"row":1,"w":6,"h":4},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"ink","expr":null},{"id":"tr1","kind":"trace","cue":"all year long","text":null,"items":null,"icon":"globe","at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"orbit","color":"blue","expr":null}]},{"id":"c3-1","narration":"In June, the northern half leans toward the sun for summer. At the very same time, the southern half leans away, so it has winter.","sig":"HUiURJms9IRbWkI5RsMstABChTFBR-VD","actions":[{"id":"june_box","kind":"box","cue":"In June","text":"June: N Summer, S Winter","items":null,"icon":null,"at":{"col":0,"row":1,"w":3,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"orange","expr":null},{"id":"a1","kind":"arrow","cue":"northern half leans","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":"orbit","to":"june_box","target":null,"color":"orange","expr":null},{"id":"hl1","kind":"highlight","cue":"very same time","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"orbit[0]","color":"orange","expr":null}]},{"id":"c3-2","narration":"Six months later in December, the opposite happens. The southern half gets summer while the northern half gets winter, giving both hemispheres opposite seasons.","sig":"SScJ2DP6QbaeJ2psreEaizAhfHSL6TxG","actions":[{"id":"dec_box","kind":"box","cue":"in December","text":"Dec: N Winter, S Summer","items":null,"icon":null,"at":{"col":9,"row":1,"w":3,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null},{"id":"a2","kind":"arrow","cue":"opposite happens","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":"orbit","to":"dec_box","target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null},{"id":"p1","kind":"pulse","cue":"opposite seasons","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"dec_box","color":"green","expr":null}]}]},{"id":"recap","kind":"recap","title":"Recap","segments":[{"id":"recap-0","narration":"Let us recap! Seasons come from Earth's tilt, not its distance from the sun. Tilt changes how directly sunlight hits us, giving opposite hemispheres opposite seasons. Do you have any questions?","sig":"dVy5Jc0HHnYwzhYmWlDlcPXdiAh5ZYkH","actions":[{"id":"recap_list","kind":"list","cue":"Let us recap!","text":null,"items":["Earth's tilt causes seasons","Direct sunlight brings more warmth","Opposite hemispheres have opposite seasons"],"icon":null,"at":{"col":1,"row":1,"w":6,"h":4},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"ink","expr":null},{"id":"recap_globe","kind":"icon","cue":"from Earth's tilt","text":"Tilted Earth","items":null,"icon":"globe","at":{"col":8,"row":2,"w":2,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null}]}]}],"quiz":[{"question":"Why is the northern hemisphere warmer in July than in January?","options":["Earth is much closer to the sun in July","The northern hemisphere tilts toward the sun in July","The sun burns hotter during July","Earth's oceans release extra heat in July"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Earth's tilt—not its distance from the sun—brings the northern hemisphere more direct sunlight and longer days in July.","chapter":1},{"question":"What happens when sunlight hits a part of Earth at a slant?","options":["It packs more energy into a small spot","It makes the days much longer","It spreads out over a wider area and feels cooler","It bounces straight back into space"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Slanted sunlight spreads the same amount of energy over a larger area, which makes the surface cooler.","chapter":2},{"question":"If it is summer in the northern hemisphere, what season is it in the southern hemisphere?","options":["Winter","Summer","Spring","Autumn"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Because Earth's tilt stays pointed the same way, when one half leans toward the sun, the other half leans away.","chapter":3}],"suggestions":["Why do we have day and night?","How do moon phases work?","What causes solar and lunar eclipses?"],"models":{"lesson":"google/gemini-4-hydrogen","voice":"google/gemini-3.8-flash-tts"},"createdAt":"2026-11-17T12:45:56.030Z","audioBase":"/samples/seasons"}